{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-555450",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $57.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $57$ are $49$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n49<57<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{49} & <\\sqrt{57}<\\sqrt{64} \\\\ 7 & <\\sqrt{57}<8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14185481/assets/2b5ce9c24d223674d04686a577347e43cd40728194596e75df7c0faa006d2888.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{57}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $7$ and $8.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=7$ and $b=8.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
